# Task 22. Without this, the build context carries the entire `target/`
# directory (multiple GB after a local build) and the generated scratch dirs,
# which is slow at best and, on a machine with a warm cargo cache, large enough
# to make `docker build` look hung.
#
# FX-53: no stage copies the whole context any more. Each compiling builder
# copies the Rust build inputs by name (`Dockerfile`'s builder stage lists and
# explains them), the runner's engine stage copies the engine's inputs, and the
# runtime stages copy their licences and anchors by name. So this file decides
# only what the context tar carries; a cache key is decided by those `COPY`
# lines. The exclusions below are kept so that no named `COPY` can reach them.
/target
/.e2e
/.demo
/.engine
/.git
/upstream
**/*.tar.gz
# PROD-00.2: the vendored engine source IS a build input. The Dockerfile's
# `engine-logweir` stage verifies it against its `.sha256` and compiles it, so
# the one tarball under third_party/ is let back in by name. It changes only
# when the pin moves, which is exactly when the engine must recompile.
!/third_party/kafka-backup-v*.tar.gz

# Task 9, carried from Task 8b's review. When the builders ran `COPY . .`
# before `RUN cargo build`, ANY file in the build context invalidated the cargo
# layer, including the two files that describe the build itself: editing a
# comment in the Dockerfile re-ran a 92-second release compile that could not
# possibly differ. Neither file is read from inside a build stage: BuildKit
# takes the Dockerfile through the frontend, not through the context tar.
/Dockerfile
/.dockerignore

# Task 23, for exactly the reason above. `Dockerfile.weirkeeper` is the second
# Dockerfile in this tree and is taken through BuildKit's frontend, not through
# the context tar.
/Dockerfile.weirkeeper

# D0 stage 7, for exactly the reason above. `Dockerfile.console` is the fourth
# Dockerfile in this tree and is taken through BuildKit's frontend, not through
# the context tar.
/Dockerfile.console

# PROD-18.1: the console's local install and build output never enter a build
# context. The console stage of Dockerfile.console runs its own `npm ci`.
/console/node_modules
/console/dist
/console/dist-single
/console/e2e-results
